WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.890
- My friends, how do you
not understand that you have

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:05.003
to produce 100 pieces of content a day?

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.280
How do you not understand that
everyday that you wake up,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.580
whether it for yourself,
or for your business,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.420
or your ambition, or your organization,

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.080
how do you not understand
that you should be putting out

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.053
50 to 100 pieces of content a day?

00:00:19.053 --> 00:00:19.886
(whooshing)

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:20.719
(popping)

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:21.552
YouTube, what's up?

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:23.450
So, so grateful that you
clicked and you're about

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.620
to watch a new YouTube
video, but before you do

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.280
212-931-5731

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.400
that is my texting community platform.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.370
I'm doing a lot of one-on-one
engagement in there

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.170
and also access opportunity,
first looks in that environment

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.870
212-931-5731,

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.010
join it now and now to the video

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.485
you've been wanting to watch.

00:00:44.485 --> 00:00:45.580
(sirens)

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.640
(men shouting)

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.361
You got your perspective.

00:00:48.361 --> 00:00:51.693
(racing techno music)

00:00:51.693 --> 00:00:53.393
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:53.393 --> 00:00:55.976
(upbeat music)

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.731
Good morning, so thank
you so much for having me.

00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:01.860
A couple of different things I wanna talk

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:02.880
about this morning.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.680
Most of all, when I think
about the makeup of the room

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.170
when you speak and you
have a general audience,

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.690
it's so important to find
the thing that most connects

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:18.280
everybody in the room.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.810
And for me

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.600
that thing very much is attention,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.420
if you really understand
what everybody here is trying

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.260
to accomplish, it's attention.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:34.260
We're very much in a place
where if you're unable

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.970
to get the attention of the end consumer,

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.260
of the person you're trying to reach,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.650
you absolutely have no chance
of actually accomplishing

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.020
the goal at hand.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.020
If you're an entrepreneur, if
you're a university professor,

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.510
if you're in the B2B space,
if you're a content producer,

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:54.753
if you're a startup,

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.690
an executive, everybody here

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.970
has to focus on actually
getting the attention

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.480
of the end consumer

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.750
and then starting to create
context for that goal.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.750
The reason I spend so much time
thinking about social media

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.920
over the last decade, is
because way back when,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.650
when this came out

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.110
and how many people here
by show of hands are over

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:24.960
40 years old?

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.100
Raise your hand.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:26.933
Thank you.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:28.723
Under 30?

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.207
Okay, so for you youngsters in here,

00:02:33.207 --> 00:02:35.050
(audience chuckling)

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:39.350
when this came out for all of us over 40,

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:43.190
I think a lot of you can remember that

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:48.080
there was a lot of debate
on how it expensive it was.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.930
Uh how many people here had
a BlackBerry in their life,

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.100
raise your hands?

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.050
Hold them up high, I want the kids

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.604
to look at all these people.

00:02:55.604 --> 00:02:56.780
(audience laughing)

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.390
Higher.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:02:59.740
Keep those lights on if you don't mind,

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.710
I like seeing the faces.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:01.840
You can put them down.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.490
Every person that just raised their hands,

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.850
when the iPhone came out
said, "I'm not getting

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.928
an iPhone, I can't feel
the buttons on the phone."

00:03:11.928 --> 00:03:14.090
(audience laughing)

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.680
That was the big debate, the
big debate the reason people

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.430
told me that they would not
get this, is because they liked

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.260
the fact that they got
used to the BlackBerry

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.360
and they could type
quickly without looking.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.420
What I understood was this
was an operating system,

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.610
that this was connected to
the single most important

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.860
invention, maybe in the
human race, or at least up

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.840
to this point, which
is called the Internet.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.650
Everybody in this room
including myself, is grossly

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.680
underestimating the impact of the Internet

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.510
on the human race.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.410
And so, you know, the Internet
started gaining real traction

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.870
on the consumer level in
the mid 90's, late 90's

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:57.830
depending on what market you're in,

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:01.670
but the reality is for me
the thing that I really

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:04.850
want to accomplish today,
is get every person from

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.710
that corner to that corner
and back to understand

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.360
that it is highly likely, highly likely,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.150
that the breakthrough, or the
advancement, or the growth

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:20.170
that you're looking for
comes at the expense

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:24.370
of you understanding that
you're actually a media company

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:25.960
and not what else you do.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.830
I believe that everybody will
realize over the next decade

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.380
that almost everything in our
society is being commoditized

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:36.110
except the ability to communicate.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:40.430
That when you think about what
you do, the product you sell

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.460
and your ability to communicate it

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.540
is fundamentally what's left.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.980
The Internet will become the wholesaler,

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.913
the distributor, the middle.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.360
There used to be enormous
value in the middle,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.930
the people that had the
middle controlled everything.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.760
The families were entrepreneurs
or media companies

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.890
that shot satellites into space,

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:07.630
or owned the printing
press or radio stations,

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.480
they had control, they distributed.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.020
Distribution was where the
leverage was because if

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:16.490
they didn't pick you, you
weren't able to see the day

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.780
of light, they were
able to keep the value.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.983
Today this is the distribution.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.720
Today

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.650
the value chain to
making something happen,

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.450
whether that is to become
the President of Mexico,

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:36.460
or to sell sneakers, the
value chain of your ability

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.800
to leverage this distribution
is the punch line.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.770
I believe that no different
than taking care of

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.350
your physical health to live longer,

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:50.340
or knowing how to balance
your check book is important

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.120
to keep your business alive,
that the new requirement

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.340
of the next decade is the ability

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.630
for you to be a communications executer.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.300
That no longer,

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.300
no longer are we in a place
where that can be outsourced

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.950
or misunderstood.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.160
Many of us in this
audience as I look around,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.470
grew up in a world where
the media had 100% control

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.030
of the narrative outside
of spending a lot of money

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:22.590
in advertising to be able to communicate.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:26.560
You know, given the way the
world's working I value,

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:28.970
especially cause I was
born in the Soviet Union,

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:33.010
I value journalism and the media
greater than ever not less,

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.520
however that does not
take out of the equation

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.340
the fact that every single
person here whether through

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:43.840
LinkedIn, a podcast,
Facebook, Twitter, Instagram,

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.860
WhatsApp, has the ability to
become their communicator.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.880
And so for me, it's been very fascinating,

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.820
it's been fascinating to
watch how many businesses

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:59.460
are getting disrupted, how many
organizations, institutions

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.320
are getting disrupted and
yet, I would argue that it

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:07.540
is highly unlikely that
what I just talked about

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.870
for the last 10 minutes is something

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.500
that is so profound for this room.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.230
I would argue what I just
spoke about for the last

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:19.010
10 minutes, is borderline common sense

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:21.480
or just reaffirmation of something

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.140
you absolutely already know.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:28.010
The important part of this
conversation, this keynote,

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:30.510
is not what I just framed up.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:34.360
That is not even a debatable aspect.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:39.230
The question is, if you agree
and understand everything

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:42.770
that I just talked about,
why are you not communicating

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.163
five to 55 times a day?

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:50.080
My question in this room
is, I don't think I dropped

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.620
any remarkable knowledge
in the last 10 minutes,

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:58.620
my question is, why am I making
at the scale that I'm making

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:02.940
everyday, why are the
people that are advancing

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.250
culturally in business, in
every arena, are the ones

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:10.970
who are actually making
content, and why are you

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:13.790
and your organization not?

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:18.300
Why are you still stuck
in a world of politics

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.910
and subjectiveness internally
to push out content?

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.830
Why are you still in a
world where you value

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:30.510
the production quality as
quality not the message

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:32.390
